3. How to Access and Run the Container on the A100 Server (Step-by-Step)
Once those files are placed on the A100 server, your guide runs the following commands:

Step 1: Load the Docker image (No internet required)
bash
docker load < llm-task-planner-image.tar.gz
Docker unpacks the image locally. Running docker images will now show llm-task-planner:latest.

Step 2: Start the vLLM Server on the A100 GPU
bash
docker run --gpus all -d \
  --name vllm-server \
  -p 8000:8000 \
  --shm-size 16g \
  -v /path/to/model_cache/Qwen3-32B:/model_cache/Qwen3-32B:ro \
  -e MODEL_PATH=/model_cache/Qwen3-32B \
  llm-task-planner:latest serve
vLLM loads the model directly onto the A100 GPU memory and serves an OpenAI-compatible API locally on port 8000.

Step 3: Access the Container Interactively (Interactive Shell)
To enter inside the container and run your experiments:

bash
docker run --rm -it --gpus all --network host \
  -v $(pwd)/results:/app/experiments \
  llm-task-planner:latest bash
You are now inside the container! You have immediate access to:

/app/scripts/run_subgoal.py (Symbolic-LLM and MCTS-LLM)
/opt/downward/fast-downward.py (Fast Downward planner)
/opt/VAL/bin/Validate (VAL plan validator)
Step 4: Run Experiments Inside the Container
Inside that container shell, simply run:

bash
# Run Symbolic-LLM on Barman domain:
python scripts/run_subgoal.py \
    --model Qwen/Qwen3-32B \
    --backend vllm \
    --domain barman \
    --subgoal_planner symbolic-llm \
    --sizes 2 3 4 5
# Or run all planners on all domains automatically:
/app/bin/entrypoint.sh run-all
